---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '~/components/Figure.astro';
import Assumptions from '~/components/Assumptions.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import Math from '~/components/Math.astro';
import Badge from '~/components/Badge.astro';
import Cite from '~/components/Cite.astro';
import RotationLab from '~/islands/RotationLab';

const assumptions = [
  { term: 'Structure', value: 'Ethanol (G2 geometry from ASE), displaced by a fixed pattern of at most 0.09 Å so that the forces are non-zero.' },
  { term: 'Distance-only model', value: 'E = Σ ½k(r − r⁰)² over the 8 covalent bonds, k = 30 eV/Å², rest lengths from the G2 geometry. Toy parameters, not a force field.' },
  { term: 'Raw-coordinate model', value: 'The same springs plus λ Σ (d·x̂)², λ = 0.6 eV/Å², where d is a bond vector and x̂ the fixed lab x axis.' },
  { term: 'Rotation', value: 'Q rotates the molecule about its centroid in the lab frame. Dragging the view moves the camera only, which changes no physics.' },
  { term: 'Units & signs', value: 'Energies in eV, forces in eV/Å, F = −∇E (arrows point downhill). Arrow length 0.3 Å per eV/Å.' },
];

const transforms = [
  { q: 'Energy E', kind: 'scalar (l = 0)', rule: 'E → E' },
  { q: 'Force on atom i, Fᵢ', kind: 'vector (l = 1)', rule: 'Fᵢ → Q Fᵢ' },
  { q: 'Dipole moment μ', kind: 'vector (l = 1)', rule: 'μ → Q μ' },
  { q: 'Stress σ, polarisability α', kind: 'rank-2 tensor (l = 0 ⊕ 1 ⊕ 2)', rule: 'σ → Q σ Qᵀ' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What changes under rotation?"
  description="Physics Lab P4: rotate a molecule and see which predictions stay fixed (energy) and which must turn with it (forces) — invariance and equivariance, with a model that breaks both."
>
  <article class="page-grid lab-page">
    <header class="wide head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/physics-lab/">Physics Lab</a> · P4</p>
      <h1 class="h-display">What changes under rotation?</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Turn a molecule in empty space and nothing physical about it changes: same bonds, same energy. Its forces still
        turn with it. A machine-learned potential has to respect both facts, and the easiest way to see why is to watch a
        model that doesn’t.
      </p>
      <div class="head-badges">
        <Badge label="toy-model" />
        <Badge label="theory" />
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="rules-h">
      <h2 id="rules-h" class="h-section">Two rules</h2>
      <p>
        For any rotation <Math tex={String.raw`\sym{Q}{Q}`} /> applied to all positions <b>R</b>, the energy must be
        <em>invariant</em> and the force on every atom <em>equivariant</em>:
      </p>
      <Math display number="1" tex={String.raw`\sym{E}{E}(\sym{Q}{Q}\mathbf R) = \sym{E}{E}(\mathbf R), \qquad \sym{F}{\mathbf F_i}(\sym{Q}{Q}\mathbf R) = \sym{Q}{Q}\,\sym{F}{\mathbf F_i}(\mathbf R)`} />
      <p>
        Below, the same ethanol molecule is fed to two toy models. One sees only bond lengths. The other also reads each
        bond vector’s component along the lab’s <i>x</i> axis, the kind of shortcut a neural network fed raw Cartesian
        coordinates can learn from data that happens to share one orientation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Rotating ethanol under an invariant and a raw-coordinate model" labels={['toy-model']} number={1} id="fig-rotation" framed={false}>
        <RotationLab client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Teal arrows are the forces each model computes for the rotated molecule, <b>F</b>(<i>Q</i><b>R</b>); thin purple
          arrows are the forces it computed before rotating, turned by <i>Q</i>. When the two coincide the model is equivariant.
          The plots sweep the full turn about the chosen axis: the distance-only model is flat at zero in both panels.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={assumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Geometry: ASE’s G2 set (<code>ase.build.molecule('CH3CH2OH')</code>). Models and parameters defined on this page.</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" id="derivation" aria-labelledby="deriv-h">
      <h2 id="deriv-h" class="h-section">Invariant energy, equivariant forces for free</h2>
      <p>
        Most MLIPs predict a single scalar energy and obtain forces by differentiation. That one design choice delivers the
        second rule from the first.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="derivation" title="Why (1, left) implies (1, right)">
        Differentiate <i>E</i>(<i>Q</i><b>R</b>) = <i>E</i>(<b>R</b>) with respect to the position of atom <i>i</i>. By the
        chain rule the left side gives <i>Q</i><sup>T</sup>∇<sub><i>i</i></sub><i>E</i>(<i>Q</i><b>R</b>), so
        <Math display tex={String.raw`Q^{\mathsf T}\,\nabla_i E(Q\mathbf R) = \nabla_i E(\mathbf R) \;\Longrightarrow\; \mathbf F_i(Q\mathbf R) = -\nabla_i E(Q\mathbf R) = Q\,\mathbf F_i(\mathbf R),`} />
        using <i>Q</i><i>Q</i><sup>T</sup> = 1. The test suite checks this numerically for the distance-only model to 10⁻¹⁰ eV/Å.
      </Callout>
      <p>
        The converse fails in practice: a model that predicts forces directly, without an energy, gets neither property
        automatically and must be built or trained to have it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="tensors-h">
      <h2 id="tensors-h" class="h-section">Scalars, vectors, tensors</h2>
      <p>Every physical output has a rule for how it turns. The rule is fixed by what the quantity is, not by the model:</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="tx">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Type</th><th>Under rotation Q</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {transforms.map((t) => (
              <tr><td>{t.q}</td><td>{t.kind}</td><td><code>{t.rule}</code></td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        <em>Invariant</em> networks build every internal feature from distances and angles, which never change, and stay
        symmetric by construction. <em>Equivariant</em> networks also carry vector and higher-order features that rotate
        like the rows above, which lets them pass directional information between atoms without breaking the rules — the
        idea behind NequIP <Cite id="batzner2022nequip" /> and MACE <Cite id="batatia2022mace" />.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="note" title="Next">
        How do you combine two directions into a new feature that still rotates correctly? That is what spherical
        harmonics and tensor products do — Physics Lab P5.
      </Callout>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lab-page {
    padding-block: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem) clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
  }
  .head .eyebrow a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .head h1 {
    margin-block: 0.6rem 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 4.4vw, 3.6rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .lede {
    font: 1.1875rem/1.6 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink-muted);
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .head-badges {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .block {
    margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font: 1.0625rem/1.7 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink);
  }
  .block p {
    margin-block: 0 1rem;
  }
  .block a {
    color: var(--atlas-arch);
  }
  .fig-wrap {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .tx {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font: 0.9rem/1.45 var(--font-sans);
    min-width: 30rem;
  }
  .tx th,
  .tx td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem 0.5rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--atlas-rule);
  }
  .tx th {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--atlas-ink-muted);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
  }
</style>
